# =============================================================================
# SUPERSET — SELF-HOSTED PRODUCTION .env   (host: ms1, macOS, Cloudflare Tunnel)
#
# Place this file at the MONOREPO ROOT as `.env`.
#   - apps/api  reads it via `dotenv -e ../../.env` in dev; in production
#     (`next start`) it must already be in the process environment, so export
#     it (e.g. `set -a; . /path/to/.env; set +a`) before launching.
#   - apps/web  same (apps/web/package.json `start` is a bare `next start`).
#   - apps/relay reads it via `--env-file=../../.env` in dev only;
#     `bun run src/index.ts` (the `start` script) does NOT load a .env file.
#   - packages/db/src/env.ts  dotenv-loads <repo-root>/.env on import.
#   - packages/auth/src/env.ts dotenv-loads `$PWD/../../../.env` — that path is
#     only correct when cwd is a package dir, so do not rely on it. Export the
#     variables into the environment instead.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY RUNS ON ms1
#   apps/api    Next.js  ->  `next start --port 3101`   (port hardcoded in
#                            apps/api/package.json)      -> superset-api.tom-nguyen.dev
#   apps/web    Next.js  ->  `next start`  (Next default port 3100; override
#                            with `next start -p <port>` or PORT)
#                                                        -> superset-app.tom-nguyen.dev
#   apps/relay  Bun/Hono ->  `bun run src/index.ts`, binds [::]:RELAY_PORT
#                            (default 3102, apps/relay/src/env.ts)
#                                                        -> superset-relay.tom-nguyen.dev
#   deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml (NOT the repo-root dev docker-compose.yml):
#                       postgres:17 (127.0.0.1:5432), neon-proxy (127.0.0.1:4444),
#                       redis:7-alpine (127.0.0.1:6379),
#                       serverless-redis-http (127.0.0.1:8079 -> container 80)
#
# NOT DEPLOYED HERE: apps/admin, apps/marketing, apps/docs, apps/mobile,
# apps/streams, apps/relay2. Their URLs still have to be *set* because api/web
# validate and use them (CORS allow-list, better-auth trustedOrigins), but
# nothing answers on them.
#
# ENV SCHEMAS THIS FILE WAS BUILT FROM (every `env.ts` that gates a deployed
# process or one of its packages):
#   apps/api/src/env.ts, apps/web/src/env.ts, apps/relay/src/env.ts,
#   packages/auth/src/env.ts, packages/trpc/src/env.ts, packages/db/src/env.ts,
#   packages/email/src/lib/env.ts
#   (also read, not deployed: apps/admin, apps/marketing, apps/docs,
#    apps/mobile/lib/env.ts, apps/discord-triage, apps/desktop/src/main/env.main.ts,
#    packages/host-service, packages/cli, packages/sdk)
#
# VALIDATION: every module above uses `skipValidation: !!process.env.SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION`
# EXCEPT packages/auth/src/env.ts and packages/db/src/env.ts, which hardcode
# `skipValidation: true`. So placeholders are enough everywhere and NO schema
# edit is needed. Do not set SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION in production unless you want
# to silence a genuine misconfiguration.
# =============================================================================


# #############################################################################
# 1. MUST SET — real values, deployment does not work without them
# #############################################################################

NODE_ENV=production

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1.1 Database
#
# CRITICAL, verified in packages/db/src/local-proxy.ts: the Neon HTTP/WS driver
# is only pointed at the local neon-proxy when the DATABASE_URL hostname is
# EXACTLY `db.localtest.me`. Any other host (localhost, 127.0.0.1, ms1.local)
# makes @neondatabase/serverless try to reach api.neon.tech over TLS and fail.
# `db.localtest.me` resolves to 127.0.0.1 via public DNS; if ms1 has no
# outbound DNS, add `127.0.0.1 db.localtest.me` to /etc/hosts.
#
# Port 4444 is neon-proxy's published host port (deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml,
# 127.0.0.1:${LOCAL_NEON_PROXY_PORT:-4444}). Port 5432 is postgres' published
# host port (127.0.0.1:${LOCAL_PG_PORT:-5432}).
#
# DATABASE_URL        -> runtime queries, through neon-proxy (HTTP + WS).
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED -> ONLY drizzle-kit (packages/db/drizzle.config.ts):
#                          `bun run db:push` / `db:migrate`. Direct to postgres.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE the host in DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED is 127.0.0.1, not localhost:
# deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml publishes Postgres as "127.0.0.1:5432:5432"
# (IPv4 only). On macOS `localhost` resolves to ::1 first, and nothing listens
# there — drizzle-kit would fail with ECONNREFUSED on ::1.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://REPLACE_PG_USER:REPLACE_PG_PASSWORD@db.localtest.me:4444/main
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgres://REPLACE_PG_USER:REPLACE_PG_PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:5432/main

# WHERE THE POSTGRES CREDENTIALS LIVE — read this before generating anything.
# The user/password/database are defined ONCE, in deploy/.env.docker
# (POSTGRES_USER / POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB), which is compose's
# --env-file for deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml. That file feeds both
# postgres.environment and neon-proxy's PG_CONNECTION_STRING, so there is one
# value, not two. Copy the SAME user/password/db into the two URLs above.
#   (The repo-root docker-compose.yml — the dev stack — does hardcode
#    postgres/postgres with no substitution. It is not what this deployment
#    runs; deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml is.)
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is deliberately NOT set in this file: no application code
# reads it (`grep -rn POSTGRES_PASSWORD` matches only the compose files), and a
# second copy here is how the password ends up generated twice and mismatched.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1.2 Public URLs
#
# Flat, one-subdomain-level hostnames (Cloudflare Universal SSL).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* are inlined into the Next.js bundles at BUILD time — see the
# BUILD vs RUNTIME section at the bottom.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://superset-api.tom-nguyen.dev
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB_URL=https://superset-app.tom-nguyen.dev

# Relay. Two names for the same service, both required by schemas:
#   RELAY_URL            server-side (apps/api, packages/trpc) + apps/web
#                        next.config.ts CSP construction.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_RELAY_URL browser-side (apps/web/src/trpc/relay-url.ts).
# Use the https:// form for both; the web client derives wss:// itself.
RELAY_URL=https://superset-relay.tom-nguyen.dev
NEXT_PUBLIC_RELAY_URL=https://superset-relay.tom-nguyen.dev

# Relay's own idea of its public address (apps/relay/src/env.ts default is
# https://relay.superset.sh — leaving it unset points the synthetic health
# check at Superset's hosted relay).
RELAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://superset-relay.tom-nguyen.dev

# Apps that are NOT deployed but whose URLs are read at runtime:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL     -> apps/api/src/proxy.ts CORS allow-list,
#                                packages/auth trustedOrigins
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MARKETING_URL -> same, plus packages/email link building
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_DOCS_URL      -> apps/web client schema (required .url())
# Pointing them at the app host is the least-surprising choice: it keeps the
# allow-lists to origins you control instead of superset.sh.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL=https://superset-app.tom-nguyen.dev
NEXT_PUBLIC_MARKETING_URL=https://superset-app.tom-nguyen.dev
NEXT_PUBLIC_DOCS_URL=https://superset-app.tom-nguyen.dev

# Web "Download for Mac" button (packages/shared/src/constants.ts). Unset, it
# points at the UPSTREAM GitHub release — a cloud-wired build. The self-host
# DMG is served by the `releases` launchd job (deploy/releases-server.ts) via
# the cloudflared /releases/ path rule; see deploy/launchd/README.md "Desktop
# update feed". Build-time: changing it needs an apps/web rebuild.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DOWNLOAD_URL_MAC_ARM64=https://superset-app.tom-nguyen.dev/releases/Superset-arm64.dmg

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1.3 Ports (server-side process binding)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apps/relay/src/env.ts: RELAY_PORT, coerced number, default 3102.
# Point the superset-relay.tom-nguyen.dev Cloudflare Tunnel ingress here.
RELAY_PORT=3102
# apps/api hardcodes 3101 in its `start` script — there is no API_PORT override
# in production (API_PORT only affects `next dev`).
# apps/web `next start` uses Next's default 3100 unless you pass -p / set PORT.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1.4 Auth
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# better-auth signing secret. Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=REPLACE_BETTER_AUTH_SECRET

# Cross-subdomain cookie domain (packages/auth/src/server.ts:170,
# `advanced.crossSubDomainCookies.domain`).
# The three hosts are SIBLINGS (superset-api / superset-app / superset-relay),
# so their only common suffix is the apex. A leading dot makes the cookie valid
# for the apex and every host under it.
# NOTE the trade-off: this sends the session cookie to EVERY *.tom-nguyen.dev
# host, not just these three. There is no narrower value that still spans them.
# CONCRETE, not hypothetical: deploy/cloudflared/README.md records an existing
# `ms1.tom-nguyen.dev` A record in this zone serving an unrelated service
# (rc-hub). Browsers will attach the Superset session cookie to requests to it,
# and to any future host in the zone. Decide whether that is acceptable for an
# instance shared by three organizations before going live; the alternative is
# moving Superset to its own zone/apex, which changes all three hostnames.
NEXT_PUBLIC_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.tom-nguyen.dev

# AES-256-GCM key for encrypted project secrets. Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32     # must decode to exactly 32 bytes
# HONESTY NOTE: `grep -rn SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY` over the whole repo matches
# ONLY .github/workflows/deploy-production.yml and deploy-preview.yml. No
# application source reads it in this checkout. Set it anyway (it costs
# nothing, and the deploy workflows treat it as required), but do not expect
# it to change runtime behaviour here.
SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=REPLACE_SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1.5 Redis / KV (serverless-redis-http in front of redis:7-alpine)
#
# These are REAL requirements, not placeholders (for the API — the relay
# stopped using Redis on 2026-09-01 when it moved to tunnel v2 with an
# in-memory host registry; see apps/relay/README.md):
#   packages/auth/src/lib/rate-limit.ts — constructs Upstash Redis at import.
#   apps/api: /mcp, /api/automations/webhook/[automationId],
#             /api/integrations/circleback/webhook/[triggerId],
#             /api/support/lookup, /api/hosts/jobs/sync-presence
#
# KV_REST_API_URL is SRH's published host port (deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml
# maps 127.0.0.1:${LOCAL_SRH_PORT:-8079} -> container 80). Use 127.0.0.1, not
# localhost: the publish is IPv4-only and macOS resolves localhost to ::1 first. Plain http:// is fine because it
# never leaves ms1 — do NOT expose 8079 through the tunnel.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KV_REST_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8079
# ONE secret under two names: this value must be byte-identical to SRH_TOKEN in
# deploy/.env.docker, which is where it is generated (compose passes it to the
# serverless-redis-http container). Generate it there, once:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# A mismatch is silent at boot and shows up as 401s from every API route that
# goes through SRH (rate limiting, webhooks, sync-presence).
# (The repo-root dev compose file uses the literal `local_dev_token`; the
#  production file requires a real value and refuses to start without one.)
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=REPLACE_SRH_TOKEN
# Raw redis wire URL. Required as .url() by apps/api/src/env.ts; nothing in the
# deployed code path dials it (the relay and auth both go through SRH), so the
# redis:// wire protocol port does not need to be reachable from the apps.
KV_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379


